270: proper reduced central fibre.  These are the specified local
271: models, with all valuation changes integral.
272: 
273: The base point \(p=0\) is ordinary.  On its smooth torus disc,
274: and on the universal logarithm cover of the scalar parameter,
275: let \(\gamma(z,v)\) be the holomorphic period of the primitive
276: suspension cycle.  Glue a second copy of this smooth torus disc
277: by the punctured-disc isomorphism
278: 
279: \[
280:  \Psi_v(\zeta,z)=
281:  \left(\zeta+\frac{\log z}{2\pi i}\gamma(z,v),z\right),
282:  \qquad \lambda=e^{2\pi i v}.
283: \]
284: 
285: A logarithm branch change adds an actual lattice period, so this
286: is a single-valued holomorphic map on the torus family, with
287: inverse given by subtraction.  The cap is smooth, its central
288: fibre has multiplicity one, and its boundary clutch is exactly
289: \(\widehat\gamma\).  This verifies the allowed logarithmic
290: transformation at an ordinary fibre even though its multiplicity
291: is one.  All pieces are proper over their closed base discs and
292: agree by the displayed holomorphic maps on collars; their finite
293: gluing is a compact Hausdorff complex threefold.
294: 
295: **Proof: integral topology.** Put \(H_i=T_i^{-t}\) on the
296: homology lattice \(\Lambda\).  The images of \(H_i-I\) have
297: Smith factors \((1),(1),(1),(1),(1,1)\).  The primitive toric
298: collapses kill exactly these vanishing lattices.  We first fix
299: an actual global origin for the auxiliary untwisted family, before
300: the ordinary logarithmic transformation.  The restriction
301: \(M|_O\) has degree \(R\cdot O-O^2=4\).  Choose its section
302: with divisor \(4t_c\).  Its nonzero values over the other base
303: points give an origin in the quotient.  Near \(t_c\), its line
304: character is \(V=q^4\) times a holomorphic unit.  Subtracting
305: four gamma periods changes this to the nonvanishing bounded
306: character \(\lambda^{-4}e^{-8\pi iC}\) times a unit and changes
307: the source coordinate by the bounded translation \(-4\Gamma\).
308: It therefore extends in the dense torus of the cusp component.
309: This proves that the origin is global, including all five singular
310: fibres.  Every base meridian based at this origin contracts in
311: its local filling.
312: 
313: Van Kampen begins with the affine torus-bundle group over the
314: punctured sphere.  After these base meridians are killed, all
315: generators lie in the abelian fibre lattice, and the conjugation
